FORTH(3)                 HIGH LEVEL EXTENSIONS TO FORTH                        FORTH(1)NAME       forth - high level forth extensionsSYNOPSIS       #include forth.f83       BITS/WORD ( -- n)       BYTES/WORD ( -- n)              MIN_INT ( -- n)       MAX_INT ( -- n)       ENTRY ( -- )       +link ( entry -- link)       +name ( entry -- name)       +mode ( entry -- mode)       +code ( entry -- code)       +parameter ( entry -- parameter)       MODES ( -- )       IMMEDIATE ( -- field)       EXECUTION ( -- field)       COMPILATION ( -- field)       PRIVATE ( -- field)       RESERVED ( -- field)       CODES ( -- )       CODE ( -- 0)       COLON ( -- 1)       VARIABLE ( -- 2)       CONSTANT ( -- 3)       VOCABULARY ( -- 4)       CREATE ( -- 5)       USER ( -- 6)       LOCAL ( -- 7)       FORWARD ( -- 8)       FIELD ( -- 9)       EXCEPTION ( -- 10)       .entry ( entry -- )       .context ( -- )       .current ( -- )       .vocabulary ( -- )DESCRIPTION       High level definitions of internal data structures. These extensions       are very implementation dependent and caution must be take as code       written using these definitions is not directly portable.       BITS/WORD ( -- n)       Number of bits per word in stack data etc. TILE Forth is 32 bits.BYTES/WORD ( -- n)       Number of bytes per word. TILE Forth is 4 bytes per word.       MIN_INT ( -- n)       Minimum integer number in two complement of 32 bits.MAX_INT ( -- n)       Maximum integer number in two complement of 32 bits.ENTRY ( -- )       Structure of vocabulary entries. +link ( entry -- link)       Field access of link to predecessor entry in vocabulary list.       Stored as a pointer, ptr, on an "ENTRY".+name ( entry -- name)       Field access of name string of an entry. Stored as a pointer,        ptr, on a null terminated string.+mode ( entry -- mode)       Field access of mode bit field of an entry. Stored as a number,       long, and access using bit fields. See "MODES" below.+code ( entry -- code)       Field access of code enumerative of an entry. Stored as a       number, enum. See "CODES" below. +parameter ( entry -- parameter)       Field access of parameter part of entry structure. Stored as       a long. May contain a pointer to the body.MODES ( -- )       Bit field structure definition of possible set of mode. The        kernel uses four bits and allows applications to use the       bits within the three most significant bytes (bit 8-31).IMMEDIATE ( -- field)       Access of immediate bit field in mode. If true then immediate.EXECUTION ( -- field)       Access of execution bit field in mode. If true then not visible       in execution mode.COMPILATION ( -- field)       Access of compilation bit field in mode. If true then not visible       in compilation mode.RESERVED ( -- field)       Access of reserved bit field in mode. These bits of the mode       are reserved for the kernel. Bits 8-31 may be freely used by       applications.CODES ( -- )       Predefined code numbers. Used by the kernel, inner, to determine       management of primitives. Codes larger than "EXCEPTION" are        regarded as pointers to forth level management code, and are        used to implement "does>"-handling.CODE ( -- 0)       Numeral for machine level code management. The parameter field       of entry is a pointer to a subroutine implementing the function.COLON ( -- 1)       Numeral for forth level code management. The parameter field       contains a pointer to the body of the definition.VARIABLE ( -- 2)       Numeral to mark variable management of entry. The parameter field       is used for the variable area.CONSTANT ( -- 3)       Numeral to mark constant management of entry. The parameter field       contains the constant value.VOCABULARY ( -- 4)       Numeral to mark vocabulary management of entry. The parameter field       contains a pointer to the latest defined entry in the vocabulary.       CREATE ( -- 5)       Numeral to mark create, symbol, management of entry.        The parameter field contains a pointer to the data area                for the symbol.USER ( -- 6)       Numeral to mark user management of entry. The parameter       field contains the offset from the task instance pointer.LOCAL ( -- 7)       Numeral to mark local variable management of entry. The       parameter field contains the offset within the current        frame to the location of the argument or local variable.FORWARD ( -- 8)       Numeral to mark forward management of entry. The parameter       field contains initially a pointer to "abort" but this is       replaced if the entry is redefined later.FIELD ( -- 9)       Numeral to mark field management of entry. The parameter       field contains the field offset.EXCEPTION ( -- 10)       Numeral to mark exception variable management of entry. The       parameter field contains a pointer to the entry..entry ( entry -- )       Given a pointer to an entry, prints all fields of the entry..context ( -- )       Similar to "words" but prints the current state of the vocabulary       search set, "context"..current ( -- )       Prints the name of the current definitions vocabulary..vocabulary ( -- )       Prints the name of the available vocabularies along the current       search path. Gives a list of available vocabularies to include.SEE ALSO       forth(1), string(1), enumerates(2), structures(2), bitfields(2)COPYING       Copyright (C) 1989 Mikael R.K. Patel       Permission is granted to make and distribute verbatim copies       of this manual provided the copyright notice and this permission       notice are preserved on all copies.       Permission is granted to copy and distribute modified versions       of this manual under the conditions for verbatim copying,        provided also that the section entitled "GNU General Pu